FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:256 — This phase added `"resume phase-073:"` to `DELIVERY_SUBJECT_PREFIXES` (lines 254-273), the rule that makes the unreviewed-work audit treat any matching commit as acknowledged with no per-commit review. `scan` resolves those commits purely by subject (`is_delivery_subject`, lines 858-862; `delivery_acknowledgements`, lines 888-901) and never inspects their contents, so a benchmark phase has edited the gate that governs its own delivery commit to auto-clear it. That is self-acknowledgement and makes the audit unfalsifiable for this phase's delivery. Concrete fix: do not let a phase add its own prefix; require the acknowledgement to be a reviewed `ACKNOWLEDGED` entry, or add a test that a prefix for the phase under review cannot be introduced by that phase. What would settle it: whether rbops requires the phase itself to register its delivery prefix (in which case this is intended and should be documented as such) or whether the audit owner is supposed to do it. Checked by reading the prefix list and the subject-only matching path.

2. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:372 — When `worker_binary()` is `None`, `measure_in_worker_with_env` silently takes the `#[cfg(test)]` fallback (`run_with_budget`) and discards both `env` and `start_marker` (lines 375-379). The "real child, killed at its budget" guarantee therefore depends on a build-layout coincidence (`CARGO_BIN_EXE_rb-bench` or the sibling `rb-bench`), not on the code under test; under `cargo test --bin rb-bench` the hidden workload runs in-process and the marker assertions in `edge_child_measurement_accepts_an_explicit_environment` fail rather than prove the child path. Fix: in `cfg(test)`, return a hard `Load`/panic when the worker binary is absent for `run_workload_with_env`, so the test cannot silently fall back.

3. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:1179 — Duplicate detection only rejects an identical `(workload, size)` pair (lines 1179-1187). A baseline carrying `fibonacci` at two different sizes is accepted, and `comparison_table` (lines 1267-1278) only requires each workload name to appear once, so `--compare` re-runs both rows and renders two rows for one workload. This contradicts the REPORT's "duplicate rows refused" claim and the checked-in-baseline invariant (one row per workload at the default size) enforced elsewhere. Fix: refuse more than one row per workload in `parse_baseline_markdown`, or explicitly document multi-size baselines.

4. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:566 — The hidden `--worker` path is user-reachable through `tests/bench/runner.rs:14-15` (`rb-bench --worker __hang <size>`), and `run_worker` sleeps `Duration::from_millis(size as u64)` with no cap and no budget (lines 563-570). `rb-bench --worker __hang 9223372036854775807` sleeps for roughly 292 million years. The flag is undocumented and test-only, but it is an unbounded resource path in a shipped binary. Fix: cap the hang size (e.g. refuse above a few seconds) or gate `--worker` behind an environment variable.

